The concepts needed to read the atlas and the forecast without misinterpreting them.
The counts describe activity observed in the catalogue, not the complete real activity: small earthquakes are detected better where and when the station network is denser. Changes between years should be read together with the evolution of the network and the processing (system change in {0}).
The other models try to improve the rate using recent activity, surroundings and depth. All are compared against the baseline; if they do not clearly beat it, they are not used.
The two most recent blocks form the final test and play no part in choosing the default model. The current model is retrained with everything before
Scenarios live only in this tab's memory (
High-magnitude events are scarce; for M ≥ {0} the differences between models are unreliable.
Large events are very scarce: for magnitude {0} or greater the models barely improve on historical frequency.
The active magnitude, depth and location filters apply to both periods. Dates are UTC days.
Filters, views and scenarios live in the tab's memory and in the browser address. Files you import are processed on your device and discarded when you close the tab.
Hexagons have equidistant neighbours, which makes it easier to measure activity in the surroundings.
The machine-learning models exist only for the trained configurations ({0}, {1} and {2} days; M ≥ {3}, {4} and {5}; all depths; full history; no smoothing).
The classification models are calibrated with isotonic regression fitted on the validation block only. So that a band with few cases does not produce probabilities of exactly {0} or {1}, each step is shrunk towards the prevalence with {2} pseudo-observations. The interface never shows {3}% or {4}%.
The periods fall on different sides of {0}, when the SGC changed the processing system and the magnitude types. Part of the difference may be due to that change and not to seismic activity.
The weights are a design decision of the project and are open to discussion. Changing them may change the recommendation when the models are very close to each other.
Historical records go back to {0}. The modern instrumental catalogue, which this platform works with, begins in {1} with the National Seismological Network.
The results are statistical associations over historical patterns. The explanations (SHAP, permutation, partial dependence) describe the model, not the physical cause of earthquakes.
The next steps are to homogenise magnitudes, fit a full ETAS model, evaluate the forecasts prospectively and, with exposure data, move towards risk. The QR code opens the atlas.
Small earthquakes are only detected where there are stations nearby. When the network grows, more small earthquakes appear in the catalogue without the Earth having changed. That is why it is always worth looking at the magnitude of completeness.
The initial values are the zone's actual values at the date of the current forecast.
